Let n ≥ 2 be a fixed integer. A real n×n matrix A is called:
• a P-matrix if all its principal minors are positive;
• weakly sign-symmetric if for every pair of index sets I, J ⊂ {1,…,n} of equal cardinality differing in exactly one index, the products A[I,J]·A[J,I] of complementary almost-principal minors are nonnegative;
• a GKK matrix if it is a P-matrix and weakly sign-symmetric;
• a τ-matrix if it is a P-matrix and satisfies eigenvalue monotonicity, meaning that for every principal submatrix B = A[I,I] one has λ_min(B) ≥ λ_min(A[I',I']) whenever I ⊆ I', where λ_min denotes the smallest real eigenvalue;
• a GKK τ-matrix if it is both a GKK matrix and a τ-matrix.

For positive integers n, k and a parameter t ∈ (0,1), let A_{n,k,t} denote the n×n Toeplitz Hessenberg matrix with entries (A_{n,k,t})_{i,j} = 0 for j > i+1, (A_{n,k,t})_{i,i+1} = 1, (A_{n,k,t})_{i,j} = c_{i-j} for j ≤ i, where the diagonals are determined by the parameters k and t in the standard Toeplitz-Hessenberg construction yielding a one-parameter family known to be GKK for all such parameters.

For a square matrix A define l(A) := min{Re(λ) : λ ∈ σ(A)}, where σ(A) is the spectrum of A. The matrix A is called stable if Re(λ) > 0 for every λ ∈ σ(A). Consider the spectral wedge inequality
   |arg(λ − l(A))| ≤ π/2 − π/n   for every λ ∈ σ(A).   (∗)

Question. Fix n. Let C_n denote one of the following classes of n×n real matrices:
 (i) {A_{n,k,t} : k ∈ ℤ_{>0}, t ∈ (0,1)};
 (ii) GKK τ-matrices;
 (iii) GKK matrices;
 (iv) τ-matrices.
Is it true that every stable matrix A ∈ C_n satisfies (∗)?

